Subject: Runs the register-ownership dataflow as an inert last encoder pass

What this does
  Runs the register-ownership dataflow as an inert last encoder pass.

Why we need it
  The register PIC, the borrowed reads and per-return release sets all need to know which physical registers may hold a reference count, and no pass computed it; the value-level form needed a guard on every drop and every guard was a hole.

What it adds
  `regvm_own_analyze` runs after `regvm_transfer_dead_sources` over the final IR on physical registers (through `regvm_reg_of`) with a NONE/OWN/MAYBE lattice and a join-based, monotone block worklist, so it can only over-approximate and it reaches the fixed point. It reports, per defining instruction, whether the destination register was count-free before the write and the owned set at every return and throw. Diagnostic only: nothing in the emitted code changes.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.
